keep the cart faces that are drawn, not eighteen

A GBA cart was OOM-killed mid-game on the handheld (exit 137, signal 9, no
core). slot was holding 22.1 MB of texture art at the time, and 7.2 MB of that
was eighteen cart faces at 312x337 -- fourteen for the current shelf and the
rest for the neighbouring shelves' visible spans -- on a device with about
60 MB free.

Upstream's FACE_AHEAD of 8 and FACE_KEEP of 12 are a GPU device's numbers: on
a shelf of fourteen carts, 12 is all of them, and shed_faces goes on keeping
them while a game is playing and no cart is on screen at all. Both become the
shelf's own drawn span, `Shelf::on_screen`, and a neighbouring shelf keeps its
selected cart rather than its whole span: nine faces where there were eighteen.

Three was tried first -- the selected cart and one either side -- and was
visibly too few within a minute of being on the handheld: the shelf draws
three either side, so the rest were flat label-coloured rectangles. The drawn
span is the smallest set with nothing missing on screen.

The two have to move together. What `face_wants` asks for is what `shed_faces`
has to keep, or the workers spend every frame building faces that are shed
before they are drawn; upstream can want a span and keep 12 because 12 is the
wider of the two, and here both are FACE_AHEAD.

The cost is a neighbouring shelf: moving to one now builds its faces as they
arrive, where upstream had them ready. That is a shelf change, not a scroll,
and it is the cheaper of the two places to spend the latency.

Upstream's three tests for this encoded the old numbers, so they are rewritten
to the same assertions against the new ones rather than deleted.

While a game is playing, nothing of the shelf is drawn, so nothing is
asked for beyond the seated cart and nothing is kept beyond it either.
That half was not in the first version of this patch and had to be, for a
reason the instrument in 0011 caught mid-game on the handheld:

    slot: faces: compose waited  100.4 ms for a cart face
    slot: faces: compose waited 1524.4 ms for a cart face
    Killed

CartFaces::sync blocks the frame loop until every face it asked for is
built, and the two face workers are the only hot threads in slot with no
priority -- audio and input are -10, emu -8, these are 0 on two cores. So
a face asked for behind a running core is a frame loop stopped for as long
as the core lets it be, which was reported from the handheld as locking up.
Launching a game is what asks for one: it reorders the Recents shelf.

It also gives the 3.4 MB back for the duration of the game, which is where
it is actually wanted.

